WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:08.500
But just as it has advantages, it surely has drawbacks. A main drawback of the accordion is that I see the data in separate pieces,

2
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:19.180
so I may not see all the data at once, and if I need to see all of it, I need many interactions, because I'll open each accordion on its own.

3
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:33.900
That's the second drawback: it raises the interaction cost. Even if I don't need them at the same time, but want data under several titles, I have to open each accordion to see what's inside.

4
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:49.240
And the last one: it hurts discoverability. Sometimes the user never finds out certain data exists, because it's inside an accordion they didn't open and didn't expect under that title, so they may never know it's there.

5
00:00:49.240 --> 00:01:03.400
So based on this, let's think about when accordions fit best. A drawback is that the user reaches the data in pieces, not all at once, and needs many interactions to see all of it.

6
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:15.180
The first good time to use accordions: when I know users won't need all the data on the page; they usually look at some of it, and there's a lot they usually don't need,

7
00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:29.600
but it's important to have it there. Then I use accordions, and users see only the data they care about. I didn't load them with a high interaction cost, because I didn't use it where users want all the data in detail.

8
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:44.840
The second good case for accordions: when the user goes through a step-by-step process. The first step sits in an accordion, and when they finish and tap Next, they move to the second step in another accordion,

9
00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:52.480
and so on, each accordion leading to the next. The user understands these are steps in order, and can reopen the first one to go back.

10
00:01:52.480 --> 00:02:08.520
Like the checkout process in front of us: I enter the shipping address, then move to the next part, delivery and gift options, and so on. Each accordion leads to the next, so this use fits.

11
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:24.380
The third case, a very good one: when the data is independent, with no piece depending on another, like FAQs. On an FAQ page the user faces a set of questions

12
00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:34.180
that aren't related; they don't need to see them all at once, or many of them at all; they need the one or two they're looking for.

13
00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:46.440
So if all the question titles are in accordions, they see every title and open the ones they care about, and skip the rest. That way we avoid the drawbacks we mentioned.

